/-!
# Foundational facts: fields, discrete valuation rings, smoothness, ampleness and depth

This file collects nine foundational results used throughout the minimal model program.

*Fields.* A countable subset `T` of an algebraically closed field `k` of characteristic zero lies
in a countable algebraically closed subfield, namely the relative algebraic closure in `k` of the
subfield generated by `T`.

*Discrete valuation rings.* For an endomorphism `u` of a finite free module `M` over a DVR `A`
with cokernel of finite length, `det u ≠ 0` and the length of `M ⧸ range u` is the valuation of
`det u`. A finite-length module is torsion, so `range u` has full rank; its Smith normal form
writes `u` as a diagonal map composed with an automorphism, so `det u = (∏ i, a i) * c` with `c`
a unit, while `M ⧸ range u ≃ Π i, A ⧸ (a i)`.

*Base change of an algebraically closed field.* For `X` locally of finite type over an
algebraically closed field `k₀` and a field extension `K / k₀`, the projection `p : X_K ⟶ X` is
flat and surjective. Reducedness and irreducibility ascend because `k₀` is perfect and separably
closed, and descend because flat local maps of stalks are faithfully flat, hence injective;
normality ascends by geometric normality and descends by faithfully flat descent. Irreducible
components correspond through `p`, and the dimension of a locally closed subset is computed as
that of a scheme and its base change.

*Smoothness and regularity.* Over a perfect field, a scheme locally of finite type is smooth at
a point exactly when its local ring there is regular.

*Upper semicontinuity of the order.* On `X` smooth over a field of characteristic zero, the
locus `{x | r ≤ ord_x I}` of an ideal sheaf `I` is closed: for `r = n + 1` it is the common
cosupport of the derivative ideals `D^j(I)`, `j ≤ n`.

*Ample twists.* If `𝒪_X(L)` is ample, then `𝒪_X(mL + E)` is ample for `m ≫ 0`.

*Depth.* Over a Noetherian local ring, a nonzero finite module is Cohen–Macaulay in the
regular-sequence sense iff its depth is the dimension of its support, and a regular element in
the maximal ideal lowers the depth by exactly one.

## Main results

* `MMP.exists_countable_isAlgClosed_subfield_superset`
* `MMP.IsDiscreteValuationRing.length_quotient_range_eq_addVal_det`
* `MMP.isReduced_isIntegral_isNormal_dim_baseChange_of_isAlgClosed`
* `MMP.mem_smoothLocus_iff_isRegularLocalRing_of_perfectField`
* `MMP.Resolution.succ_le_multAt_iff_forall_mem_support`
* `MMP.Resolution.isClosed_setOf_le_multAt`
* `MMP.Mori.eventually_isAmple_cartierSheaf_nsmul_add`
* `MMP.Mori.isCMModule_iff_localDepth_eq_supportDim_and_isCohenMacaulayModule`
* `MMP.Mori.localDepth_quotSMulTop_add_one`

## Implementation notes

* The valuation of the fraction field of a DVR `A`, restricted to `A`, is the additive valuation
  `IsDiscreteValuationRing.addVal A : A → ℕ∞`; the module `A ^ r` is generalised to an arbitrary
  finite free module, and instead of diagonalising a matrix by row and column operations we use
  the Smith normal form `Submodule.smithNormalFormCoeffs` of `range u`.
* Orders of ideal sheaves take values in `ℕ∞` (`MMP.Resolution.multAt`), so the threshold `r`
  ranges over `ℕ∞`; a real threshold `r ≥ 0` gives the same locus as `⌈r⌉`. For the converse
  inclusion we use the Euler derivation `E = ∑ tᵢ δᵢ` built from derivations `δᵢ` dual to a
  regular system of parameters `t`: on `𝔪ᶜ` it acts as multiplication by `c` modulo `𝔪ᶜ⁺¹`, so
  in characteristic zero some `δᵢ f` has order exactly one less than `f`.
* Ampleness of a Cartier divisor `D` is ampleness (`Stacks.Ample.IsAmple`) of its invertible sheaf
  `MMP.CartierDivisor.sheaf D`, and "for `m ≫ 0`" is `∀ᶠ m in Filter.atTop`. No
  Noetherian hypothesis is needed. Rather than proving that an ample sheaf tensored with a
  globally generated one is ample, we split off from `mL + E` the power `kᵢ L` whose section is
  given by ampleness, so that only one tensor product of two sections is formed.
* Depth is `MMP.Mori.localDepth`; the drop `depth (N/xN) = depth N - 1` is stated additively,
  `depth (N/xN) + 1 = depth N`. The zero module is Cohen–Macaulay by convention in
  `MMP.Mori.IsCMModule` but not for `IsCohenMacaulayModule`, so the comparison assumes `N ≠ 0`.

## References

* [J. Kollár, S. Mori, *Birational geometry of algebraic varieties*][KollarMori1998]
* [J. Kollár, *Lectures on resolution of singularities*, §3]
* [Stacks Project, Tags 00TV, 01PS, 01PV, 01Q3, 038V, 0B8X]
-/

